{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787164335,"nonce":"sOQ0Th/EM57NfRodrgcKLbyiDBU=","message":"**Fixed in `3dd8a7d`, on `main`.**\n\n`validate:\"required,http_url\"` on `HTTPConfig.BaseURL`, plus an explicit control-character check in `Validate`.\n\n**The choice this ticket asked to have recorded: `required` AND KEEP THE DEFAULT.** Not \"required with the default removed\" — the `http://localhost:8080` default preserves the zero-config dev path, and what needs refusing is an explicitly wrong value rather than an absent one. A Helm value that renders blank overrides the default with `\"\"`, so `required` still catches the realistic failure.\n\n**`http_url` rather than `url`**, which answers the scheme question raised in **Scope**: `url` admits `oto.example.com` with no scheme. Slack will not linkify that — the card renders, the link does nothing, and nobody finds out.\n\n⭐ **AND THE TAG ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, which the tests proved rather than assumed.** Writing them surfaced an asymmetry I would otherwise have shipped: `\\n`, `\\r` and `\\t` fail the `http_url` tag, but **`|`, `\u003e`, `\u003c` and a plain SPACE pass it** — Go's URL parser is permissive about them. Those four are exactly the ones from comment #1 that corrupt a Slack link. Had I trusted the tag, the whole point of that comment would have gone through unguarded.\n\nSo there is a second, explicit check, written in the CORS-wildcard check's own shape — a rule that needs a sentence gets one. It names the offending character and its byte offset, because an operator reading a startup failure about a URL that *looks* fine has nowhere else to go.\n\n⚠️ **`link()` is untouched, as comment #1 argued it should be.** It escapes the label and never the URL, and that must stay: the Alertmanager silence deep link is legitimately percent-encoded and escaping it would mangle it. One boot check fixes every case instead.\n\n**Tests — `base_url_test.go`, six:**\n- the shipped defaults still validate — otherwise every zero-config start breaks\n- blank and whitespace-only refused (the original defect)\n- scheme-less, protocol-relative, path-only and `ftp://` refused\n- all seven control characters refused, **with the two distinct refusal paths pinned and explained** so the next reader knows why both mechanisms exist\n- ordinary values accepted, including a percent-encoded path — the shape `link()` must not escape\n\n**Gate:** the eleven `ci` recipes at `3dd8a7d`, `dirty_before 0` / `dirty_after 0`. All eleven byte-identical to the measured baseline: `lint-vocabulary` exactly 60, one `--- FAIL` (`TestNoStateHueInChrome`, pre-existing from `59c7def`). Zero drift.\n\n**What this does NOT fix, stated so it is not assumed:** the empty-`base_url` path that made every card's deep link vanish is now unreachable by configuration, but nothing yet asserts at the RENDER boundary that a card which should carry a link does. If `Links.Group` is ever empty for a different reason, the same silent degradation returns. That is a smaller, separate guard and it is not filed.\n\nRelated: `1cd496f`, closed — it makes an over-long base URL degrade instead of killing the delivery. Neither subsumes the other.","files":null}]}